# <upstream-url> [<deployed-sha>]
# Records what is deployed. Never triggers a pull — see bin/check-sources.
#
# NO SHA HERE, DELIBERATELY. This stack is *registry-deployed*: nothing is built on
# `two` (a 700 MHz single core), so there is no checkout on the box for a sha to name.
# The image is built for linux/arm/v6 by the upstream repo's own CI and published to
# ghcr.io/gsfernandes81/destiny-director.
#
# And there would be nothing stable to write down even if there were somewhere to write
# it: compose.yaml names a MOVING BRANCH TAG (`:dev`), and every deploy pulls it
# unconditionally, so a deploy takes whatever that branch points at then. A sha
# in this file would go stale the next time anyone pushed, silently, and nothing on
# either side could catch it — the shape of pass-for-the-wrong-reason docs/decisions.md
# ends on. The two facts are one design, not two exceptions: the deploy deliberately
# follows a branch, so the pin deliberately does not exist.
#
# WHAT IS RECORDED WHERE, since the answer is split:
#   which line of code    compose.yaml, tracked here — the one knob, a literal tag
#   which commit          only the box. `podman inspect dd-beacon --format '{{.Image}}'`
#   which deploys ran     nowhere. The dispatcher that kept an audit log is gone; a
#                         deploy is now a shell command in the deploy user's history.
#
# So the line below records provenance, and only that. bin/check-sources recognises a
# URL-only SOURCE and reports the stack as `registry-deployed` — not drift, not
# malformed, and it does not affect the exit status. A second field that is not a sha, a
# third field, or a first field that is not a URL is still a hard failure there: absence
# of a pin is the design here, but a garbled SOURCE must never pass for one.
https://github.com/gsfernandes81/destiny-director.git
